Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Find OHCI controllers on expansion cards

OHCIDriver finds controllers by asking the machine's HAL what USB hardware
exists, via HAL_USBControllerInfo. HAL_IOMD has no USB support at all, so on a
Risc PC or A7000 nothing is ever found, however real the controller is. A module
cannot supply the missing HAL entry either: OS_Hardware adds a device, but HAL
entries live in the ROM, and HAL_IOMD's entry table ends at HAL_IRQMax, which
hdr/HALEntries fixes at 107 - the USB entries begin at 108, past the end of the
table rather than in a spare slot.

This adds a third discovery stage, after PCI and the HAL have both come up
empty, which searches the expansion cards. A card is identified by what is in
it rather than by where it is: every card declaring EASI space is asked for its
controller revision at +&800000, and the one answering &10 is taken to be an
OHCI controller. An E<slot> command tail narrows the search to a single slot.

The interrupt is claimed with the card's own status byte and bit rather than the
podule manager's IntRequest and IntValue. Those are hardcoded to IOMD's shared
registers in the Kernel's s/Tables, and PIRQ_Despatch in s/NewIRQs calls only
the first claimant whose bit is set, jumping rather than returning - so a driver
claiming the shared registers matches every podule interrupt in the machine and
starves the other cards. This is the fault that makes PHCIDriver wedge any
machine with a second card fitted. The address OS_ClaimDeviceVector needs is the
podule manager's CombinedAddress rounded down to a 16K boundary: the combined
address is where the card's ROM starts, not where the card starts.

Two consequences of a copy carried in a card ROM rather than started from disc:
an unrecognised command tail no longer refuses to initialise, because a card ROM
starts its modules with a tail of its own choosing; and ResourceFS registration
is now unconditional and tracked by a flag, because a card-carried copy is the
first instance in every way except that r11 is not zero.

Also: ohci_init's return value was discarded. When it fails the module still
goes Active with a half-built softc, hands that softc to USBDriver, and the first
call through a method table ohci_init never finished filling in takes the
machine down somewhere unrecognisable. It now refuses to stay resident.

Baseline: OHCIDriver 0.56, tag OHCIDriver-0_56, commit 551b4047
           https://gitlab.riscosopen.org/RiscOS/Sources/HWSupport/USB/Controllers/OHCIDriver

Apply from the root of that checkout with:  patch -p1 < <this file>

